\title{Author Response --- Round 3}
\author{}
\date{}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

We thank the reviewers for their detailed and constructive feedback. We have revised the paper to address each concern. Below, we respond point-by-point, noting specific changes and their locations.

\section*{Overview of Changes}

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
\item New title scoped to observed evidence rather than general claims.
\item Abstract and Introduction: Bonferroni-adjusted $p = .100$ now immediately qualifies the 79\% reduction at first mention.
\item All instances of ``general property'' replaced with empirically scoped language.
\item \S4.5 expanded to detail the GDN head-count confound in the main text.
\item ARR Responsible NLP Research Checklist added.
\item Table~1 footnote explains the $p$-value rounding discrepancy.
\item Limitations section strengthened with single-checkpoint caveat.
\item \texttt{wang2025hybrid} bib entry corrected (title, authors).
\end{itemize}

\section*{Detailed Responses}

\subsection*{W-CITE (R2-N1): Incorrect citation for model zoo}

\reviewer{The citation for the model zoo (wang2025hybrid) has incorrect title and author list.}

\response{Fixed. The bib entry now reads ``A Systematic Analysis of Hybrid Linear Attention'' with authors Dustin Wang, Rui-Jie Zhu, Katharopoulos, and Gu.}

\location{\texttt{references.bib}, entry \texttt{wang2025hybrid}.}

\subsection*{W-CHECKLIST (R2-N2): Missing ARR Responsible NLP Research Checklist}

\reviewer{The submission lacks the mandatory ARR Responsible NLP Research Checklist.}

\response{Added. The checklist covers all sections (A1--A4, B1--B6, C1--C3, D, E) with specific justifications. Notably, C1 reports the computational budget (single NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000, $\sim$5 GPU-hours inference-only) and A3 explicitly notes the scoped claims.}

\location{\texttt{arr\_checklist.tex}, included after the Ethics Statement in \texttt{main.tex}.}

\subsection*{W-OVERCLAIM (R1-W1, R2-N3): Overclaiming on convergence}

\reviewer{The 79\% reduction is presented prominently without immediately noting that the adjusted p-value does not reach significance. The title claims ``Task-Specific Convergence'' which may overstate the strength of evidence.}

\response{Three changes address this:}
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=2em]
\item \textbf{Title}: Changed from ``Task-Specific Convergence and Per-Example Predictive Multiplicity in Hybrid Recurrent Architectures'' to ``Per-Example Predictive Multiplicity in Hybrid Recurrent Architectures: Evidence from Two Benchmarks and Three Backbones.'' The convergence claim is removed from the title.
\item \textbf{Abstract}: At first mention of the 79\% reduction, we now write ``though the Bonferroni-adjusted $p = .100$ does not reach conventional significance.''
\item \textbf{Introduction}: The opening hook (79\% reduction) is immediately followed by the same Bonferroni qualification.
\end{enumerate}

\location{Title (line 1 of \texttt{main.tex}); Abstract (paragraph 1); Introduction \S1 (paragraph 1 and Finding 1).}

\subsection*{W-GENERAL (R1-W2): ``General property'' overclaims from limited evidence}

\reviewer{Claiming per-example multiplicity is a ``general property of hybrid architectures'' is too strong given only three architectures and two benchmarks.}

\response{We have replaced all instances of ``general property'' with empirically scoped language and expanded the per-example analysis from two to three benchmarks (plus one supplementary):}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=2em]
\item Per-example analysis now covers three benchmarks spanning three task categories: LAMBADA (retrieval), HellaSwag (reasoning), ARC-Easy (mixed). BoolQ (comprehension, binary classification) is reported as supplementary in the Appendix due to structural limitations affecting Jaccard significance.
\item Multiplicity is characterized as a task-dependent spectrum: $\alpha$ ranges from 0.10 (HellaSwag) to 0.30 (LAMBADA).
\item HellaSwag exhibits the lowest multiplicity ($\alpha = 0.10$), confirming that multiplicity magnitude is task-dependent rather than noise.
\item Within-benchmark $\alpha$ stability verified for all three benchmarks: range 0.012 (HellaSwag), 0.025 (ARC-Easy), 0.022 (LAMBADA), all below 0.03 (Appendix, Table~11).
\item Conclusion ensemble claim: now qualified with ``if confirmed in additional architectures''
\end{itemize}

\location{Abstract, \S1, \S3.4, \S4.3 (Table~2), \S4.4, \S5, Limitations, Appendix.}

\subsection*{W-GDN (R1-W4): GDN head-count confound}

\reviewer{GDN's h3-1 configuration uses 16 attention heads rather than 32, creating a confound. The sensitivity analysis is only in the appendix.}

\response{\S4.5 (Robustness) now contains two paragraphs. The first retains the existing sensitivity test: removing GDN weakens $\rho$ from $-1.0$ to $-0.7$ ($p = .233$), while the RetNet--HGRN2 pair preserves the decoupling (all hybrid $\sigma \leq 0.008$, Jaccard saturates at 0.086--0.108). The second paragraph, newly added, explains the mechanism: GDN's h3-1 uses 16 attention heads vs.\ 32 in all other configurations, introducing an attention-capacity asymmetry. We state the conclusion explicitly: ``The aggregate convergence result is therefore partially dependent on GDN; the decoupling result is not.''}

\location{\S4.5, paragraph 2.}

\subsection*{R3-POWER (R1-W3): Low statistical power ($n = 5$)}

\reviewer{With $n = 5$ attention ratios, the minimum achievable p-value is $\sim$0.008, limiting the power of the convergence test.}

\response{We acknowledge this as a structural constraint of the model zoo design. It is discussed in three places: (1) the Method section describes the exact permutation test with 120 permutations; (2) \S4.1 reports the BCa bootstrap CI $[-1.0, -0.7]$ as a complementary characterization alongside the $p$-value; (3) the Limitations section (``Statistical power'' paragraph) explicitly states the minimum achievable $p$-value ceiling and its consequences. We agree that this limits the strength of the convergence finding, which is why we have removed convergence from the title and weakened all convergence-related claims (see W-OVERCLAIM above). The per-example multiplicity finding, which is the paper's central contribution, does not depend on the convergence $p$-value.}

\location{\S3.5, \S4.1 (paragraph 1), Limitations (``Statistical power'' paragraph).}

\subsection*{R3-BINARY (R1-W5): Binary correct/incorrect decomposition}

\reviewer{The helped/hurt decomposition uses binary correctness, which may lose information compared to continuous confidence scores.}

\response{The binary decomposition is a deliberate methodological choice aligned with the predictive multiplicity framework of Marx et al.\ (2020), which defines multiplicity over discrete prediction outcomes. Additionally, we report continuous-valued analyses that are consistent with the binary results: Cohen's $d$ for pure-model confidence margins ranges from 0.73 to 0.92 between helped and hurt examples on LAMBADA (Appendix, ``Confidence Margin Analysis''), showing that the binary decomposition captures a real separation in the continuous confidence space. We have extended this analysis to all four benchmarks:}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=2em]
\item \textbf{Rescue pattern (C2)}: Near-miss errors rescued by attention replicate across all three main benchmarks (9/9 tests, $d = 0.48$--$0.77$, all $p < 10^{-9}$). On BoolQ (binary classification), the rescue signal is weaker but directionally consistent (2/3, $d = 0.16$--$0.36$).
\item \textbf{Disruption pattern (C1)}: Effect sizes decrease with answer-space dimensionality: open-ended prediction ($d = 0.83$--$0.92$, LAMBADA) $>$ four-way multiple choice ($d = 0.09$--$0.28$, HellaSwag/ARC-Easy) $>$ binary ($d \approx 0$, BoolQ). This gradient is consistent with compressed log-likelihood ranges in constrained-choice settings reducing the confidence margin's predictive power.
\end{itemize}
The Jaccard similarity measure applied to the binary sets provides an interpretable metric of cross-architecture overlap that would be less transparent with continuous alternatives.

\location{\S4.3 (confidence margin paragraph), Appendix (Confidence Margin Analysis, Cross-Benchmark Extension, BoolQ Supplementary Analysis).}

\subsection*{W-ZOO (R1-W6): Reliance on a single model zoo}

\reviewer{All results depend on one model zoo; the controlled design limits external validity.}

\response{We acknowledge this trade-off explicitly. The controlled design of the zoo---identical training data, tokenizer, and hyperparameters across all configurations---is what allows us to isolate architectural effects from training confounds. This is stated in \S3.1 and in the Limitations section (``Architecture coverage'' paragraph), which now also notes: ``Each configuration in the model zoo corresponds to a single training run; we cannot disentangle architectural effects from seed-specific effects.'' The Conclusion calls for extending the analysis ``beyond these three architectures.'' We believe the controlled design is the appropriate choice for a first systematic study, while recognizing that confirmation across additional model families is needed.}

\location{\S3.1, Limitations (``Architecture coverage''), \S5.}

\subsection*{Q1: p-value rounding discrepancy (0.100 vs.\ 0.102)}

\reviewer{Why does the Bonferroni-adjusted p-value appear as both 0.100 and 0.102 in different places?}

\response{We have added a footnote to Table~1 explaining the discrepancy: ``Bonferroni-adjusted $p = 6 \times 2/120 = 0.100$ (exact); 0.102 arises from rounding raw $p$ to .017 before multiplying.'' The value 0.100 is the exact result; 0.102 is a rounding artifact. We now use 0.100 consistently throughout.}

\location{Table~1 footnote.}

\subsection*{Q5: Single checkpoint per configuration}

\reviewer{Each model zoo configuration is a single training run. Can you disentangle architecture from seed?}

\response{We cannot, and we now state this explicitly in the Limitations section: ``Each configuration in the model zoo corresponds to a single training run; we cannot disentangle architectural effects from seed-specific effects.'' This is a limitation of the zoo design. Disentangling the two would require multiple seeds per architecture-attention configuration, which is beyond the scope of the available zoo. We note, however, that the zoo's controlled design (shared data, hyperparameters) reduces other confounds, making architecture the most salient axis of variation.}

\location{Limitations (``Architecture coverage'' paragraph).}

\subsection*{W-REFS (R2-W2): Additional related work citations}

\reviewer{Several relevant references are missing from the related work discussion.}

\response{We have substantially expanded the citation coverage. In addition to Sellam et al.\ (ICLR 2022; MultiBERTs) and Fehlauer et al.\ (EMNLP 2025) added in Round~2, we now cite five additional references:}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=2em]
\item \textbf{Predictive Multiplicity paragraph}: Du et al.\ (2025; reconciliation under multiplicity), Haghighat et al.\ (2026; Rashomon-set resolution), and Gorbett \& Jana (2026; cross-model disagreement as label-free correctness signal).
\item \textbf{Hybrid Architectures paragraph}: Ali et al.\ (2025; implicit attention in Mamba) and Lahoti et al.\ (2026; Mamba-3).
\item \textbf{Appendix~D}: Ali et al.\ (2025) cited as methodological background for attention weight analysis.
\item \textbf{\S4.3}: Gorbett \& Jana (2026) cited for the practical relevance of per-example disagreement.
\end{itemize}

\location{\S2 (``Predictive Multiplicity'' and ``Hybrid Recurrent Architectures'' paragraphs), \S4.3, Appendix~D, \texttt{references.bib}.}

\end{document}

\section{Experiments}\label{sec:experiments}

We analyze how attention affects cross-architecture performance variance using models from the hybrid recurrent model zoo of \citet{wang2025hybrid}. Our analysis spans three architectures (GatedDeltaNet, RetNet, HGRN2), five attention ratios (0\% pure recurrent, 4\%, 7.7\%, 14.3\%, 25\%), and two scales (340M and 1.3B parameters). In the h$k$-1 notation used throughout, one attention layer is interleaved per every $k{+}1$ layers; h3-1 denotes 25\% attention. All models within each scale share identical training data and hyperparameters; they differ only in architecture and attention configuration. We evaluate on 10 benchmarks spanning retrieval, comprehension, reasoning, and mixed task categories, plus one supplementary benchmark (COPA, $n = 100$).

\subsection{Task-Specific Convergence}\label{ssec:task_convergence}

We first ask whether adding attention layers reduces performance differences between architectures, and whether any such reduction is uniform across tasks. For each benchmark, we compute the cross-architecture standard deviation ($\sigma$) at each attention ratio and measure the Spearman rank correlation ($\rho$) between attention ratio and $\sigma$ across the five configurations.

Table~\ref{tab:convergence} reports results for all benchmarks at 1.3B scale. LAMBADA is the only benchmark exhibiting monotonic convergence: $\rho = -1.0$ (exact permutation test, $p = .017$, two-tailed, $n = 5$, 120 permutations). Cross-architecture $\sigma$ drops from 0.024 to 0.005, a 79\% reduction. The 95\% BCa bootstrap confidence interval for $\rho$ is $[-1.0, -0.7]$ (10{,}000 resamples), which excludes zero. After Bonferroni correction for six confirmatory benchmarks, the adjusted $p = .100$ does not reach conventional significance at $\alpha = 0.05$; the confidence interval provides a complementary characterization indicating a reliably negative association.

LAMBADA is a pure context-retrieval task \citep{paperno2016lambada}; attention provides direct token-level access to the full context, consistent with convergence reflecting compensation for differences in how recurrent architectures store contextual information. No other benchmark shows significant convergence (all $|\rho| \leq 0.7$, $p \geq .233$). ARC-Easy and MMLU exhibit moderate negative correlations ($\rho = -0.7$) but non-monotonic trajectories; COPA shows a similar pattern ($\rho = -0.67$) but is limited by $n = 100$ (Figure~\ref{fig:task_specificity}). The decoupling analysis (\S\ref{ssec:decoupling}) is demonstrated on LAMBADA; per-example divergence extends to all three benchmarks with per-example analysis (\S\ref{ssec:per_example}).

